        "If you think cybercriminals only target large corporations, think again. 43% of all cyberattacks target small businesses, and 60% of those businesses close within six months of a breach. Your website is often the most exposed part of your digital infrastructure — and if it's not secured properly, it's an open door for attackers. Here are the essential security practices every small business website needs in 2026.",
        "## SSL Is the Bare Minimum — Not a Feature",
        "An SSL certificate (HTTPS) encrypts data between your website and visitors' browsers. In 2026, not having SSL isn't just a security risk — it's a search ranking penalty. Google Chrome marks HTTP sites as \"Not Secure,\" instantly destroying visitor trust. SSL should be included in every website as a standard feature, not an upsell. If your web agency charges extra for SSL, that's a red flag about their practices.",
        "## Keep Your Software Updated — Always",
        "The most common attack vector for small business websites is outdated software. Unpatched CMS platforms, plugins, and server software contain known vulnerabilities that attackers exploit automatically with bots. If you're running WordPress with 15 plugins that haven't been updated in 6 months, you're essentially leaving your front door unlocked. Custom-built websites with minimal dependencies have a significantly smaller attack surface.",
        "## Implement Strong Authentication",
        "Brute force attacks against login pages are constant and automated. Protect admin areas with strong passwords (minimum 16 characters), two-factor authentication (2FA), and rate limiting that blocks repeated failed login attempts. Never use default credentials, and restrict admin access to specific IP addresses where possible. A compromised admin panel gives attackers complete control of your website.",
        "## GDPR Compliance: Legal and Ethical Obligation",
        "If your business serves European customers, GDPR compliance isn't optional — it's law. This means clear cookie consent mechanisms, transparent privacy policies, secure data storage, the ability for users to request data deletion, and breach notification procedures. Fines for non-compliance can reach €20 million or 4% of annual revenue. But beyond legal risk, GDPR compliance signals that your business takes customer privacy seriously — building trust that translates to conversions.",
        "## Secure Your Contact Forms",
